WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.610
Before you watch this video, to make sure
you’re not a robot,

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.660
please look at these pictures and identify
the street signs, the cars,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:06.920
the fire hydrants, the shop fronts,

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.010
the numbers on the front doors,

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.009
and the ever-growing sense of frustration.

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:18.120
In the late 90s the most popular search engine
was AltaVista, and they had a spam problem.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.960
People were writing automated scripts to send
in spam and malicious links to their

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.080
finely crafted database of the web,

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.190
and they needed to add a test that
only humans could pass.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.190
Their solution was to add a question to the
submission form that,

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:31.949
back then, only a human could answer.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:35.820
In their case it was to identify a warped
string of letters and numbers.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.340
Image processing wasn’t at the point where
a computer could easily identify the squiggly letters,

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.880
but it was trivial for a human.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.710
Or at least,
trivial for a human with good eyesight.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.700
Accessible versions didn’t
come along for a while.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.240
But that was one of the first public versions of what
became known as a CAPTCHA;

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.480
a Completely Automated Public Turing Test to Tell Computers and Humans Apart.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.290
A version called reCAPTCHA came along a few
years later:

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.590
it was used to help scan old books and newspapers.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.320
When a CAPTCHA was needed,

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.530
the team would send one scanned word that
they knew was right, deliberately distorted,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.490
and another word that their scanning systems
weren’t sure about.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.970
The user would have to type in both:

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.750
the known word was to check it was a human
answering, they had to get that right,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.720
but the unknown word – after maybe a dozen
people had agreed on what it was –

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.260
that would be logged as part of the book scan.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.450
Google ended up buying reCAPTCHA.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.830
But by then, the arms race was well underway.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.810
The bot makers looked at reCAPTCHA as a challenge,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.280
and they rose to it admirably.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.890
First, they could train computers
to read those messed up words.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.550
This was before the recent breakthroughs in
machine learning and artificial intelligence,

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:44.350
but even a fairly rudimentary system could
solve reCAPTCHA well enough to let bot-makers

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.159
create fake accounts and send spam
some of the time.

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.650
If the test was still too difficult, though,
they could just pay humans.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.850
The bot makers set up systems where
automated bots would fill in all the details,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.920
ready to send spam, and then
when the CAPTCHA appeared,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.210
the bots would show it to human operators,

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.720
hired from countries where
the average income is low.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.670
Those humans got paid to sit there and solve
CAPTCHA after CAPTCHA after CAPTCHA.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.689
After all, it’s only testing that there’s
a human in the loop somewhere.

00:02:12.689 --> 00:02:16.500
You could even outsource your CAPTCHA solving
needs to any one of dozens of companies

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.060
who all competed on price and accuracy.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.770
Actually, you still can.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:24.290
Or you could get unsuspecting members of the
public to solve CAPTCHAs for you.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.079
You could set up a web site with, er,

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:29.400
some images that some people
might want to see(!)

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.230
and  before they could visit,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.220
they’d have to prove they were human by
solving a CAPTCHA.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.680
Which was copied straight from whatever site
your bots were trying to get into.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.159
So then Google released
reCAPTCHA version 2.

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:44.090
Which is where you’re presented with a single
check box,

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.310
that you have to click on to prove you are
not a robot.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.950
And clearly, any bots presented with that box
would be honest and not click it.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.459
It’s not really about clicking the box.

00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:58.069
When you complete one of these new CAPTCHAs,
extra data is sent.

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:00.120
And Google is very cagey about what
that data is,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.849
because everything they reveal is a clue for
the people trying to break it.

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:06.939
But that box is loaded into your browser from
google.com,

00:03:06.939 --> 00:03:10.419
which means it can look at any login cookies
that Google already have on your browser.

00:03:10.419 --> 00:03:11.790
Certainly if you clear your cookies,

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.320
you are way more likely to get
that secondary check

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.459
that asks you to identify
buses or fire hydrants.

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:20.310
And maybe it checks how your mouse moves in
the moments before clicking the box?

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.370
And the exact position and length of time your
finger tapped the phone screen?

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:28.669
Plus a bunch of other things that Google all feeds
into their giant machine-learning system.

00:03:28.669 --> 00:03:33.579
The only people that know for sure
are the designers, and they aren’t telling.

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.209
The CAPTCHA solving services, of course,

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:37.290
are already offering a cost per thousand to
solve these.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.620
It may be harder,
but it’s not unbreakable.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:40.620
Using machine learning,

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.639
bots can be trained to pass those secondary
checks themselves, and to hide as humans,

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.760
well, identifying the correct sections
of the presented images

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.380
is something that you can throw
cloud machine learning at.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.220
And given that Google Cloud sells
machine learning systems,

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.870
it’s very likely that some of their servers
are creating CAPTCHAs,

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.150
and others are trying to break them.
And, even then,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.520
you can just have humans on standby instead.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.480
So at the end of 2018,
Google released reCAPTCHA version 3.

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:08.519
And you might have already passed, or failed,
one of those without knowing it.

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:11.739
There’s no box to tick, no puzzles to solve:
when you browse round a site,

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:15.000
version 3 works in the background
and watches what you do.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.709
By the time you’re posting a comment or
signing up,

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:21.389
it’s already assigned you a score
based on how likely you are to be human.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:24.729
And again, Google is being very careful
about saying how they’re working that out.

00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:28.740
But the answer is very likely “it's a machine learning system they're throwing everything into

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.650
"and they  don’t know it works either”.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:33.889
Hopefully they’re taking account of
incognito mode, and accessibility tools.

00:04:33.889 --> 00:04:35.340
Because if you get a low score,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.639
maybe your comment will get sent to a 
moderator to check… or maybe it’ll just

00:04:39.639 --> 00:04:41.960
disappear into nothing and you'll never know.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.470
The bot makers, of course,
are already working on the challenge.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.069
I signed up for a new bank account online
a few months ago,

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:53.620
and I had to send in a photo of my ID and a
video of me holding that ID and waving.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.830
That’s checking not just that I’m a human,

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.629
but that I’m the one specific human
I claim to be.

00:04:58.629 --> 00:05:02.050
Bots are becoming more and more indistinguishable
from humans.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.080
Successful CAPTCHA methods are having to be
more and more intrusive.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.949
The arms race continues,

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:10.530
as it has done for
twenty years or  more.

